## Public Route Notes
- **pim**
  GET /public/pim/items preserves today's shared-catalog behavior for pilot: when app_id is present, rows with app_id null/missing can still be returned by default.
  Use include_shared=0 on GET /public/pim/items to request app-owned rows only.
  Read response.scope.scope_mode, response.scope.requested_app_id, and response.scope.shared_catalog_included before assuming strict app-only visibility.
  The MCP handoff contract intentionally documents the stable CRUD surface here. The mounted public PIM router can also expose additional workflow, template, and status helpers for operator or app-specific handoffs; do not assume PIM is CRUD-only if a targeted handoff references extra routes.
  PUT /public/pim/items/:id/publish sets publish_state (draft|published|revoked) and visibility grants (public, app:<id>, tenant:<id>). Products default to draft with no visibility grants on create.
  GET /public/pim/published/items returns published products visible to the caller. Optional ?source=<publisher_app_id> reads directly from that app PIM DB. Without source, the platform uses platform_shadow_products to discover publishers and hydrates from each app DB (visibility enforced server-side).
  PUT /products/:id rejects requests containing PII field names (email, phone, first_name, last_name, registration_number, postal_address, physical_address, etc.). Store personal data in entity registry collections only.
  DAM writes (digital_assets) now route through getAppDb() — app_id in the upload JWT determines which MongoDB the asset metadata lands in. Object storage (Spaces CDN URLs) is unchanged and globally accessible regardless of app ownership.
  Memory writes (entity_memories) now route through the app-aware resolveMemoryDb(). Memories created via POST /public/memories land in the calling app's DB. Platform/control-plane scoped memories (app_id: control-plane, api_pipeline:*) still land in the shared store.
  Relationships (POST /public/relationships) now write to RELATIONSHIP_DB_NAME via resolveRelationshipDb(). This matches the DB that entity-memory-service reads during graph traversal, fixing a correctness bug where propagateMemory() could miss edges.
- **contextualizer**
  Contextualizer operator routes live on /api/* and /cp/contextualizer/* rather than /public/* because they are governed build-time surfaces, not browser-runtime CRUD. All /cp/* routes require JWT.
  Standards and template access for app instances is exclusively through contextualizer-registry Volcano workflows (ctx-template-*, ctx-iku-* step templates). Direct MongoDB access to standards data is not permitted.
  If you change Contextualizer route contracts, update both dc__get_api_routes and the app capability manifest in the same change so external App Instances do not build against stale assumptions.
  Pipeline v5.1 ensure_template uses 3-way routing: confidence >= 0.85 → resolve_existing, 0.4-0.84 → enhance_existing (adds attributes to existing template, bumps version), < 0.4 or null match → author_new (creates and persists new template). The action field in the response indicates which path ran.
  enhance_template guarantees zero attribute removal: the Sonnet draft must output the full merged attribute array. Template ID is never changed. Version minor is always incremented.
  author_template is now self-persisting: it uses $setOnInsert with upsert:true so concurrent calls for the same entity type produce exactly one template (race-safe).
  Template IDs MUST be resolved from the contextualizer registry via ctx-template-get or ctx-template-resolve, dc__discover_app_schema, dc__propose_template_binding, or dc__pim_upsert_smart / dc__entity_upsert (classification + authoring gate). Do NOT invent template IDs. Never call contextualizer-pipeline-v5 from app instances.
  Template binding confirmation: dc__pim_upsert_smart and dc__entity_upsert enforce a mandatory structural binding gate on create — unbound writes (template_id missing or unconfirmed) are rejected fail-closed. When classification resolves a template that has not been confirmed for your app, the tool returns proposal_required directing you to dc__propose_template_binding → dc__confirm_template_binding. dc__confirm_template_binding writes Built-artifact units to entity_registry when target_plane is entity_registry or the template kind is Built-artifact — iku-products does not override that door (spec templates still go to PIM). Apps can configure template_confirmation.mode to "auto" or "threshold" in settings to bypass first-use confirmation only after a binding is established. Call dc__list_pending_proposals at session start to check for orphaned proposals.
  Template authoring gate: ALL new template mints persist as pending_review until dc__confirm_template_authoring promotes them (single activation authority). dc__pim_upsert_smart, dc__entity_upsert (no template_id), and dc__propose_template_binding return authoring_proposal_required when classification would mint — no entity write occurs. Review via dc__list_pending_template_authoring, promote with dc__confirm_template_authoring, or reject with dc__reject_template_authoring. Confirm (and mint/amend) never keeps a brand+model or other particular title as template.name when class_instance_dissent / class_instance_wall is PARTICULAR or mint_blocked — the name is forced to the generic concept label (from concept_slug / entity_type) or confirm refuses.
  Authoring fail-closed: any governed authoring failure (find_or_amend_template error state, walker/model-fill abort, amend JSON blobs) returns a clean error and persists ZERO template rows at any status — no placeholder or error-derived template births.
  Governed amend_template (contextualizer-registry): in-place attribute upserts + required-set only. Identity fields and status are immutable. Persist is getTemplateCollection() upsert:false after a correction snapshot. Shared templates (not visibility=app_scoped with a single created_for_app_id) are Core/platform-seat only and always propose→confirm. App seats may amend only their own app-scoped templates, using the app template_confirmation.mode. Every persist stamps actor (seat + caller) and the confirmation reference. rollback-correction restores only a snapshot whose prior_doc hash-matches template_correction_snapshots for that template id (ROLLBACK_UNVERIFIED_PRIOR_DOC otherwise). Writer-closure stays closed — do not route amends through Volcano mongo_insert on template_registry.
  Shape law at classification: before mint, no-hint classification compares proposed attribute shape against same-kind actives (threshold SHAPE_EQUIVALENCE_THRESHOLD=0.88). Same kind + equivalent shape + different values = VALUE_VARIANT — resolves to existing template, never authors.
  Lifecycle vocabulary: assets RETIRE (derived records from owns); entities DISSOLVE (waiting room, never a home). Both are deliberate platform lifecycle states.
  rel:instance_of hook edge: created automatically with owns derivation (asset→product lineage); cascade-cleaned when owns edge is deleted.
  Placeholder identity lint: template names, ids, concept slugs, and type_keys containing {{ are rejected with placeholder_id_rejected at authoring and binding doors. Relationship propose/confirm tests BOTH endpoint legs.
  Recommended bootstrap flow: (1) dc__describe_platform → understand capabilities, (2) dc__discover_app_schema → get your data model blueprint with templates, schemas, relationships, and plane routing, (3) dc__propose_template_binding per entity → confirm each template binding, (4) dc__pim_upsert / dc__entity_upsert → create entities with confirmed templates. Relationships (when present in schema): dc__propose_relationship_binding → dc__confirm_relationship_binding (first-use gated) → relationship-manager.semantic_upsert; list edges with dc__relationship_list or GET /public/relationships; verify counts via the total field.
- **api_ingest**
  GET /public/api-ingest/stored-values is LATEST + a short series per owned lane. It is the Home card door. It is not an issued-forecast archive.
  Issued multi-day forecasts emit event:forecast.snapshot.persisted on instance_ingest success (Pulsar domain.events.<tenant> → Timescale forecast_snapshots). Query GET /public/api-ingest/forecast-snapshots or dc__query_forecast_snapshots by issued_at / valid_for. Health pings and current/observation lanes do not persist. LATEST stored-values is unchanged.
  Compare and score: GET /public/api-ingest/forecast-snapshots/compare|score and dc__compare_forecast_snapshots / dc__score_forecast_snapshots. Actuals are Open-Meteo historical (default) or this app's linked station observations. Score requires caller metrics — there is no default global metric. Missing data is honest empty, never invented.
  Do not use a browser snapshot-ring as source of truth for accuracy / #1 / streak / days-scored.
- **weather**
  GET /public/weather/current resolves current conditions through canonical API Ingest weather providers (Google Weather, Open-Meteo, MET Norway) and only falls back to OpenWeather for legacy app runtimes.
  Callers can provide either city or latitude/longitude. When only city is provided, the platform geocodes it before hitting coordinate-based weather providers.
  Use response.weather as the stable normalized current-weather payload. response.data remains the raw API Ingest preview payload for debugging and provider-specific iteration.
- **events**
  Browser/runtime twin of dc__emit_event and dc__query_events. Same validation, Pulsar domain.events.<tenant> publish, and Timescale domain_events query. Not a second events system.
  Auth: Contact JWT or App JWT with entity:read (query) or entity:write (emit). ev_* may query the bound room only (same empty as a miss when the subject is missing or foreign). Wrong/missing auth returns JSON 401/403 — never Kong "no Route matched with those values" once this door is live.
  event_type must be an ACTIVE standards-plane event_types id (e.g. event:job.posted). Unknown or inactive types return honest JSON (event_type_not_found / event_type_not_active), not a router 404.
  Demand-style lists (posted/accepted/cancelled): GET /public/events?event_type=event:job.posted,event:job.accepted,event:job.cancelled (comma-separated) or event_types as a repeated query/body array. Do not invent a /demand Core route — that page is overlay; it queries this door.
  Do not put vertical job fields in Core. Attributes and ref_slots come from the event type registry / overlay.
  event:job.posted allows optional attributes.test (boolean) as a sandbox marker. Persist test:true on the event, never in the job name. Other job.* types are unchanged; existing events are not auto-tagged.
  Structure writes (entity upsert, PIM products, relationship edges) are receipted in platform_audit_log door-log, NOT domain_events. Cite door_log_id from write responses; verify with dc__get_door_log_entry / dc__list_door_log (platform:read, own app only).

## Direct Mongo Guidance
- Storage mode: `shared`
- All MongoDB access from your app goes through DC agents with mongodb MCP steps. Create agents via dc__create_agent and call them via /public/agents/execute. NEVER use MongoClient or mongoose in application code.
- App-scoped MCP Mongo routes automatically resolve the correct database from app context.
- Public REST agent endpoints (/public/agents/execute, /public/agents/chat) also resolve the dedicated database automatically — no special headers needed from the frontend.
- Agent sub-steps (agent provider calling another agent) propagate the dedicated DB URI through the full execution chain. A data-reader agent delegating to contextualizer-registry will use the correct dedicated DB.
- Do not pass database names or connection strings in app-scoped MCP calls.
- Collections are created on first insert.
- Use App Instances to configure dedicated Mongo; MCP never returns the raw URI.

## Available Platform Tools
- `dc__describe_platform` — Describes the DevCockpit platform, your app, available agents, public API routes, data models, authentication, Standards Plane guidance, discoverable step-template patterns, and top-level app/MCP scopes. Call this first to understand what you can do. For binding-specific runtime scopes, inspect dc__get_data_connection.
- `dc__plan_architecture` — Ask Agent Manager to map your app requirements to DevCockpit platform services and persist a contract draft before you build. Planning does not seed allowlist entries or data-source bindings.
- `dc__export_app_contract` — Returns the current persisted app contract if one exists, plus the current archetype, latest validation state, and available archetypes. Fresh apps may return contract: null until planning or scaffolding persists a baseline.
- `dc__validate_app_contract` — Runs preflight validation against the persisted app contract, checking bindings, scopes, routes, standards prerequisites, and agent runtime expectations. If no contract exists yet, persist one first via dc__plan_architecture or dc__scaffold_app_domain.
- `dc__scaffold_app_domain` — Apply an archetype scaffold that seeds bindings, allowlist entries, standards guidance, and a contract baseline for this app. Use this after planning when validation gaps still exist.
- `dc__app_status` — Returns this app's current status, provisioning state, builder plan summary, and hosting info.
- `dc__get_data_connection` — Returns safe runtime connection metadata for this app, including storage mode, public API bases, enabled data-source bindings, binding-specific runtime scopes, Standards Plane / provenance guidance, and memory guidance. These binding scopes can be narrower than the top-level app scopes shown by dc__describe_platform. Secrets are never returned.
- `dc__list_agents` — Lists all agents on this app's allowlist with their descriptions and available workflows. Legacy single-workflow agents are surfaced as a default workflow so they do not disappear from discovery. Treat this as the canonical callable set for app-scoped execution.
- `dc__get_agent` — Fetch the full agent definition from the agent registry for an app-owned agent. Returns volcano_config, deployment stage, tags, and lifecycle metadata.
- `dc__list_agent_executions` — Query execution history for an app-owned agent from the agent registry execution-history store.
- `dc__tool_schema` — Returns the live input schema and source details for a specific tool name (platform or agent workflow).
- `dc__get_data_schema` — Returns JSON schema / field definitions for a given data type (entity, asset, pim_product, infrastructure).
- `dc__get_api_routes` — Returns the public REST API routes that your generated frontend should call, with auth instructions. Static-export frontends should use these /public/* surfaces instead of inventing local Next.js API routes for secret-bearing production flows.
- `dc__create_app_api_key` — Mint an ak_* API key for the calling app (builder surface — same as CP App Instances → Create API Key). Returns the full key once; exchange via POST /auth/app-token with x-api-key for App JWT on /public/* routes.
- `dc__exchange_app_token` — Mint a short-lived App JWT for /public/* self-tests. Without api_key: signs from MCP session scopes (builder self-test). With ak_* api_key: exchanges via POST /auth/app-token (production path).
- `dc__store_memory` — Stores an app-scoped memory record using a canonical entity_id such as app:<app_id>, asset:<id>, field:<id>, legal_entity:<id>, or api_pipeline:<id>.
- `dc__recall_memories` — Recalls all memories for a specific canonical entity_id (e.g. "app:app_123", "asset:uuid", "field:field_42"), optionally including related-entity memories. Requires entity_id — for general text search without an entity scope use dc__search_memories instead.
- `dc__search_memories` — Full-text search across this app's stored memories.
- `dc__search_memory_events` — Search grouped event-driven memories by event_id, event_type, or memory content.
- `dc__get_memory_corroboration` — Load all memories, related entities, and graph corroboration for a single event_id.
- `dc__deploy_app` — Returns deployment instructions for this app. Deploys go to SANDBOX by default (sandbox.devcockpit.ai/apps/<app_id>/). Pass target=production to deploy directly to production. Hosted deploys auto-inject env-config.js with ENVIRONMENT, APP_BASE_PATH, and a runtime error overlay.
- `dc__promote_app` — Request promotion of the current sandbox frontend build to production. Creates a PENDING promotion (10-minute expiry) — does NOT copy files until dc__confirm_promotion is called after human review of the sandbox URL.
- `dc__confirm_promotion` — Confirm a pending promotion after the developer has reviewed the sandbox build. Copies sandbox to production and re-injects env-config.js with ENVIRONMENT=production.
- `dc__deploy_backend` — Triggers a one-click backend deploy for this app (GitHub Actions workflow-dispatch). Writes backend_deploy_* events into platform_audit_log and stores desired backend config in config_settings.settings.backend. This exposes an app backend/API route and does not replace the hosted frontend path at /apps/<app_id>.
- `dc__set_custom_domain` — Binds a custom hostname to this app's hosted zip for production or sandbox. Writes an nginx vhost (www + apex when the name is the registrable domain), persists settings, and returns the exact DNS A records. TLS is Cloudflare Universal SSL (orange-cloud + Full, not Strict) — not certbot. Pass confirm_tls=true after HTTPS works. {app_id}.devcockpit.ai is a different feature.
- `dc__configure_hosted_self_signup` — Enables or rotates hosted browser self-signup for this app. Generates a non-secret public registration client_id, stores the hosted signup policy, and injects the client_id into window.__DEVCOCKPIT__ on the next deploy. New-user browser flow: Firebase sign-up/login -> POST /public/identity/auth/register-token -> POST /public/identity/auth/register -> POST /public/identity/auth/exchange. Returning Google/email users skip register-token and call exchange only; a register-token 403 with fatal_for_signin:false is not a sign-in failure.
- `dc__get_firebase_config` — Returns the public Firebase web SDK config when the server can reconstruct the same FIREBASE_WEB_* / hosted env-config.js inject used for window.__DEVCOCKPIT__. Not the only Firebase source of truth. Hosted apps should read window.__DEVCOCKPIT__ and prove login on the hosted URL after deploy inject. An empty or inject_only payload is not "Firebase disabled" if those inject fields exist on the app record or hosted env-config.js.
- `dc__api_ingest_chat` — Start or continue the async API ingest workflow for this App Instance. Use this supported platform tool instead of raw api-ingest-orchestrator__* workflows from app-scoped MCP. The first call creates a session and returns immediately with session state; poll dc__api_ingest_session_get until you reach a terminal state such as activated or failed.
- `dc__api_ingest_session_get` — Poll an API ingest session started by dc__api_ingest_chat. Returns normalized status, workflow_state, next_action, validation, provenance, and credential wait states so you can resume async API setup safely.
- `dc__query_forecast_snapshots` — Query this app's issued forecast archive (not LATEST stored-values). Filter by issued_from/issued_to and valid_for/valid_from/valid_to so Weather can drive #1 / streak / days-scored without a browser snapshot-ring. Empty is honest when no snapshots match. Requires api_ingest:read.
- `dc__compare_forecast_snapshots` — Compare issued forecast days to observed actuals for the same day/location. actuals_source: open_meteo_historical (default when no station observations), station (this app's current/observation ingest), or auto. Does not invent forecasts or actuals — missing actuals return actuals_missing:true. Requires api_ingest:read.
- `dc__score_forecast_snapshots` — Rank forecast providers by the caller's preferred metric(s) (precip, wind_speed, temp_min, temp_max, …). No default global metric — omitting metrics returns empty_reason:metrics_required plus available_metrics. MAE / days_scored / streak use only days where both forecast and actual exist. Requires api_ingest:read.
- `dc__list_pipelines` — Lists API ingest pipelines visible to this app. Each entry includes pipeline_id, alias (e.g. nasa.planetary_apod), provider, endpoint_id, api_id, and enabled state. Use the alias when calling dc__get_pipeline_by_alias instead of memorizing pipe_<sha> ids. Phase 3 (Platform Contract v1).
- `dc__get_pipeline` — Fetch full pipeline detail for a pipeline_id owned by this app. Returns purpose/run_kind, enabled, next_run_at, vault fallback fields, last_error, last_run, last_success, request.query, and alias when registered. Use this instead of dc__list_pipelines when you already have the pipeline_id (e.g. from ingest setup). Returns pipeline_not_found (404) or pipeline_not_app_owned when the id is foreign.
- `dc__health_ping_pipeline` — Run an explicit health_ping against an app-owned pipeline using stored Vault credentials. Optional lat/lon apply at request time only — they are never persisted and never flip purpose to instance_ingest. Does not enable the scheduler. Returns run_id immediately; poll dc__get_pipeline for last_error (vault_credential_fallback:* vs upstream_error:*) and vault fallback timestamps. Do NOT use dc__run_pipeline for connector health checks.
- `dc__get_pipeline_by_alias` — Resolve a pipeline alias (provider.endpoint, e.g. metos.station_data) to its pipeline_id and full pipeline detail for this app. Returns 409 if the alias points at a deleted pipeline. Phase 3 (Platform Contract v1).
- `dc__update_api_endpoint` — Change the stored path (or absolute url) of one endpoint on an existing external API. Does not remint the API, does not rewrite templates, and does not touch credentials or Vault. Runtime joins base_url + path — use this when mint stored a product-root path. Requires api_ingest:write and an API owned by this app or an owned pipeline on that api_id.
- `dc__update_pipeline` — Update an API ingest pipeline configuration. Change the polling interval (schedule_interval in seconds), store_mode (telemetry/api_telemetry/both/pim/domain_events), ingest_mode (pulsar/timescale/dual), or enabled state. domain_events emits governed domain events on the mapping relationship subject (e.g. weather readings on a Place twin). Use dc__list_pipelines to get the pipeline_id first. Changes take effect on the next scheduled run. Does not set request.query — use dc__update_pipeline_query for instance lat/lon.
- `dc__update_pipeline_query` — Persist request.query on an app-owned pipeline (instance lat/lon and provider query keys) via PATCH /api/pipelines/:id/query. Use this when dc__update_pipeline cannot set query. Optional enabled flips the scheduler only when the query already looks like an instance geo query.
- `dc__run_pipeline` — Trigger an immediate one-off instance ingest run of an API ingest pipeline, bypassing its schedule. Returns a run_id immediately; the ingest runs asynchronously. Optional run_kind selects instance_ingest vs health_ping. For connector health checks with request-time coords, prefer dc__health_ping_pipeline.
- `dc__delete_pipeline` — Hard-delete an API ingest pipeline owned by this app. Refuses pipelines whose app_id does not match the calling app (tenant-wide deletes are not allowed via MCP). Use dc__list_pipelines first. Irreversible — prefer dc__update_pipeline with enabled:false to pause. Ops UI fallback: Data Pipeline → Data Flow Manager → Delete Pipeline.
- `dc__dam_ingest_url` — Ingests a remote document/image URL into DAM for this app: downloads the URL, uploads to Spaces, creates the asset, and triggers DAM processing (async).
- `dc__pim_upsert` — Create or update a PIM product for this app. template_id MUST be a registered contextualizer template (use ctx-template-get to verify). Writes to the correct products collection (dedicated or shared), emits shadow sync, and returns the saved product. Use this instead of raw MongoDB inserts to ensure shadow sync, template validation, and cross-app discovery.
- `dc__pim_upsert_smart` — Create or update a PIM product with automatic template resolution. If template_id is omitted, the contextualizer pipeline classifies the entity and resolves or creates the appropriate template before writing. Use this when you do not know the template_id upfront.
- `dc__entity_upsert` — Create or update an entity in the Entity Registry. Use this for non-product entities such as legal entities, farms, animals, infrastructure, environmental records, or compliance documents. Requires entity_type (e.g. "legal:company", "animal:cattle", "infrastructure:dam"). Template is resolved automatically from entity_type if template_id is not provided.
- `dc__propose_template_binding` — Propose a template binding for an entity before committing it to a data plane. Runs template classification (read-only for entity data) and returns a structured proposal with schema, relationships, confidence score, and a human-readable markdown summary. Call dc__confirm_template_binding to commit or dc__reject_template_proposal to reject. No entity data is written until confirmation.
- `dc__confirm_template_binding` — Confirm a previously proposed template binding and commit the entity to the correct data plane (PIM products or Entity Registry). Records template usage, snapshots the schema version, and emits an audit event. Verification from the consuming seat: after confirm, re-read the entity via dc__entity_get / dc__pim_get (or the plane you wrote) and treat template_usage_recorded=false as incomplete even if success=true.
- `dc__reject_template_proposal` — Reject a template binding proposal. No entity data is written. The rejection reason is recorded as a training signal for the contextualizer.
- `dc__propose_relationship_binding` — Propose a relationship (edge) binding before committing it. Resolves endpoint kind/iku from entity_registry or the PIM products record (Specification/Class catalog rows included), honors explicit from_iku/to_iku (and from_kind/to_kind) when provided, then checks the standards-plane relationship_types contract. Returns a structured proposal. No edge is written until dc__confirm_relationship_binding. A missing or illegal shape is author-gated (relationship_binding_unresolved) and NEVER auto-authored.
- `dc__confirm_relationship_binding` — Confirm a previously proposed relationship binding. This registers the binding (proposal) for your app — stamps relationship_template_id, records usage, and emits an audit event — but does NOT write the edge: the response carries persisted:false and edge_exists:false. The edge is materialized only by the single edge writer, relationship-manager__semantic_upsert (or POST /public/relationships). An app that stops at confirm has written nothing.
- `dc__relationship_list` — List relationship edges for this app. Mirrors GET /public/relationships with filters and total count. Requires relationships:read scope.
- `dc__relationship_delete` — Delete a relationship edge by Mongo _id. Governed removal for bad or orphan edges — same auth as DELETE /public/relationships/:id (relationships:write scope).
- `dc__list_pending_proposals` — List template binding proposals for this app. Call at session start alongside dc__get_platform_deltas to check for orphaned proposals from previous sessions. Defaults to pending proposals only.
- `dc__confirm_template_authoring` — Promote a pending_review template to active after human review. Required before low-confidence authored templates become resolvable or bindable. If class_instance_dissent marks PARTICULAR / mint_blocked, confirm force-renames template.name to the generic concept label (from concept_slug / entity_type) or refuses — brand+model titles stay on the instance, never the type.
- `dc__list_pending_template_authoring` — List templates awaiting authoring review (metadata.status pending_review). These templates are invisible to resolution until confirmed.
- `dc__reject_template_authoring` — Remove a pending_review template that should not enter the Standards corpus.
- `dc__discover_app_schema` — Discover the shared public ontology and data model available to your app. Call with no arguments to see the full shared core_global/public template set. Optionally narrow with domain, entity_hints, or archetype. Returns matching templates with attribute schemas, relationships, and plane routing (PIM vs Entity Registry). Call this BEFORE creating entities to understand what the platform already models.
- `dc__emit_event` — Emit a governed, schema-validated, timestamped domain event about a structural twin. Events are NOT entities — refs (performed_by, vaccine_batch, etc.) are carried by the event and validated against event_types ref_slots, not graph edges. Publishes CloudEvent to domain.events.<tenant> via platform gateway (apps never write Pulsar directly). Requires an ACTIVE event type in the standards-plane event_types registry. Committed event:room.note.posted also fans out envelope-only wakes to bot member seats (never the author); wake JSON has secret-redacted text_preview ≤240 plus full secret-redacted text.
- `dc__query_events` — Query governed domain events from TimescaleDB domain_events hypertable (timestamped facts emitted via dc__emit_event only). Structure writes (entity upsert, PIM products, relationship edges) are NOT in this store — use door_log_id + dc__get_door_log_entry. Filter by subject or entity_id (matches subject_id or any ref slot), event_type, time range, or ref slot.
- `dc__get_door_log_entry` — Read one platform door-log receipt from platform_audit_log by door_log_id. Returns status (pending | failed | committed). Strict app scoping: returns the entry only when it belongs to the calling app seat; cross-app ids are refused as door_log_not_found. Requires platform:read.
- `dc__list_door_log` — List recent platform door-log receipts for the calling app only (platform_audit_log). Each entry exposes status (pending | failed | committed). Strict app scoping — no cross-app reads. Requires platform:read.
- `dc__evaluate_compliance` — Evaluate deterministic compliance verdicts (COMPLIANT / NON_COMPLIANT / PENDING) over recorded domain events. Read-only — never blocks emit. Only ACTIVE compliance_rules judge. R3/R4 evaluate at the event occurred_at, not wall-clock now.
- `dc__handoff_post` — Posts a short handoff note for this app into DevCockpit. The note body and tags are persisted in platform_audit_log and a cursor_handoff event is emitted for observability. Use this to coordinate between multiple Cursor instances working on the same app. Refuses when the session app is not active. Verification from the consuming seat: the chain is not done until that seat's dc__handoff_list shows the note.
- `dc__handoff_list` — Lists recent handoff notes for this app from DevCockpit. Returns persisted message, tags, timestamp, and author_key_id for recent cursor_handoff notes. Archived/resolved messages are hidden by default.
- `dc__ops_handoff_list` — Operator tool: lists recent handoff notes for any app in this tenant. Requires platform:ops scope on the calling key. Pass the target app_id explicitly. Use this to read another app's mailbox as a platform operator.
- `dc__ops_handoff_post` — Operator tool: posts a handoff note into any app's mailbox in this tenant. Requires platform:ops. Identify the live receiving seat first (active app_id from their dc__handoff_list / dc__describe_platform) — refuses inactive/deleted targets unless allow_inactive_target=true. Verification from the consuming seat: done only when that seat's dc__handoff_list returns the note.
- `dc__create_agent` — Create a new app-scoped agent. Two modes: (1) Manifest-backed â€” provide name, description, domain, and capabilities without volcano_config. Agent Creator composes a proposed workflow and returns a manifest with actions for dc__refine_agent, dc__commit_agent, dc__test_dry_run, and dc__discard_manifest. (2) Explicit â€” provide name, description, and volcano_config with a full workflow definition for deterministic immediate creation. Explicit mode auto-adds the agent to this app's allowlist. Manifest mode does not create an agent until dc__commit_agent succeeds. Prefer explicit volcano_config for production agents. For browser-facing bridge workflows, reject outputs that return processing/continue, unresolved template placeholders, or internal_data:null when the frontend expects synchronous JSON. Canonical volcano_config top-level shape: { workflows: { default: { steps: [...] } }, default_workflow: "default" }. Use provider-specific step fields, not ad-hoc tool/input_map shapes.
- `dc__refine_agent` — Update refinable fields on an uncommitted agent manifest. Allowed changes are name, description, and capabilities. Re-runs capability-to-skill resolution and validation.
- `dc__commit_agent` — Commit a manifest-backed agent to agent_definitions. Re-validates skill versions, persists the agent, and returns bootstrap_status plus ready_for_execution. Pass retry_bootstrap:true to retry a previously pending bootstrap.
- `dc__test_dry_run` — Validate and expand a manifest-backed workflow in Volcano dry-run mode without executing provider side effects.
- `dc__discard_manifest` — Discard an uncommitted manifest-backed agent proposal.
- `dc__promote_agent` — Promote an app-owned agent to a new deployment stage using the supported app-scoped lifecycle path. Use the `to_stage` field. Promotions are linear: draft -> staging -> production, with demotions back one stage when needed. Do not call raw agent-registry lifecycle workflows from app MCP.
- `dc__archive_agent` — Archive an app-owned agent. This is the supported app-scoped lifecycle path; do not call raw agent-custodian archive workflows from app MCP.
- `dc__restore_agent` — Restore a previously archived app-owned agent. This is the supported app-scoped lifecycle path; do not call raw agent-custodian restore workflows from app MCP.
- `dc__update_agent` — Partially update an app-owned agent in the agent registry. Supports description, tags, workflow changes via volcano_config/workflow, and ai_config (pass null to clear a stale model pin — runtime already ignores legacy ai_config). volcano_config.workflows are MERGED with existing workflows by default (siblings stay intact). Pass replace_workflows:true only when intentionally replacing the full workflow map. Prefer this over creating duplicate agents when refining an existing definition.
- `dc__delete_agent` — Hard-delete an app-owned agent from the agent registry. Requires agents:write and app ownership. Irreversible — prefer dc__archive_agent for normal retirement.
- `dc__execute_agent` — Execute an allowlisted agent workflow through the supported MCP Gateway -> Volcano path. Internal visibility and allowlist rules are re-checked server-side; do not call raw Volcano HTTP execute routes from app-scoped MCP. Markets live ingest (dry_run:false) returns HTTP 202 + run_id — success:true with an empty body is a fail. Poll the same agent with workflow "poll" and input.run_id.
- `dc__get_platform_deltas` — Returns recent platform changes (agents registered/updated, templates created/updated, Yoda rules seeded, deploys) that affect this app or are global. Call at the start of a session to check for changes since your last work. Unread deltas may require action â€” check breaking: true entries first.
- `dc__list_platform_learnings` — List already-distilled platform learnings (operational_memory.learnings). Requires platform:read. Returns insight text, owning agent, category, and the promotion gate (min_distinct_tenants stays 3). Does not return customer feedback rows, top_apps, or another seat's data. Empty is honest — not Processing. Setup-class scars are not in this store. Cross-app PIM still needs a grant and is a different surface.
- `dc__get_platform_learning` — Get one already-distilled platform learning by id. Requires platform:read. Missing id returns learning_not_found (not an empty list). Does not return customer feedback rows.
- `dc__create_sandbox` — Create an isolated sandbox copy of an existing app instance for development and testing. The sandbox gets its own database and MCP key but inherits the source app agent allowlist and template usage index. Template authoring is blocked in sandbox mode. Sandboxes expire after ttl_days (default 30).
- `dc__list_sandboxes` — List existing sandbox app instances. Optionally filter by source app. Returns sandbox metadata including expiry and source app.
- `dc__delete_sandbox` — Delete a sandbox app instance, drop its isolated database, revoke its MCP key, and clean up all sandbox metadata. Only works on sandbox apps â€” cannot delete production apps.
- `dc__asset_list` — List assets from the Entity Registry. Supports pagination, filtering by type/status/owner/IMEI, and link expansion.
- `dc__asset_upsert` — Create or update an asset in the Entity Registry. Provide id to update an existing asset. Supports digital twin fields: serial_number, imei, owner_entity_id, pim_product_id, data_stream.
- `dc__asset_provenance` — Get the full provenance chain for an asset: Owner â†’ Product â†’ Asset â†’ Data Stream, plus platform relationships. Returns chain_completeness (full/partial/orphaned).
- `dc__iot_device_register` — Register an IoT device in the canonical iot-shack devices collection (pim-db.devices) so it appears in the farmer-portal IoT page and the /public/iot/devices public API. Use this instead of dc__entity_upsert or dc__pim_upsert for anything with an IMEI, serial number, MQTT topic, or physical installation location. Idempotent within the caller app partition: if a device with the same deviceId already exists for this tenant and app_id, returns the existing record with existing:true; otherwise creates a new one and returns created:true. If the same deviceId is registered under a different app_id, returns HTTP 409 conflict (not existing:true) — cross-app ownership must be resolved by platform ops. Writes land under {tenant: app_tenant_id, app_id: app_id} partition keys from MCP context.
- `dc__iot_device_update` — Patch product/unit/subject pointers on an app-owned IoT device without re-registering. Register is an idempotent create (same deviceId → existing:true, no overwrite). Use this door to unset or rebind productId, sku, templateId, and/or subject_entity_id. Empty or null means unbound. IMEI/deviceId identity changes are refused (DEVICE_IDENTITY_IMMUTABLE). App seats cannot update another app's device (device_not_owned). Writes an actor-stamped door-log entry. Requires iot:write.
- `dc__refresh_app_dev_from_prod` — Copy production data into sandbox for this app with PII anonymisation. Reads all collections from app_{id}_prod, applies field-level PII hashing/redaction (emails hashed, free-text redacted), and writes the anonymised data into app_{id}_sandbox. Requires per-app dual-DB provisioning (storage_mode=dual). Safe to call multiple times â€” each invocation replaces sandbox data entirely.
- `dc__provision_app_databases` — Provision per-app dual databases (sandbox + prod) on the Instance cluster for an existing app. Creates app_{id}_sandbox and app_{id}_prod databases. Idempotent â€” safe to call on apps that already have dual databases. Updates config_settings to storage_mode=dual.
- `dc__pim_list` — List PIM products for the current app. Supports pagination, search, template filtering, and sort options.
- `dc__pim_get` — Get a single PIM product by id.
- `dc__pim_revoke_legacy` — Lifecycle-only revoke for a PIM row that physically lives in the caller's own store but whose template now routes to entity_registry (legacy mis-route). Two-phase door-log: intent pending → publish_state flip → committed. Failed intent aborts the flip. Requires pim:write.
- `dc__pim_delete` — Delete a PIM product by id.
- `dc__pim_revoke_legacy` — Lifecycle-only revoke for a PIM row that physically lives in the caller's own store but whose template now routes to entity_registry (legacy mis-route). Sets publish_state to revoked without changing content fields or deleting the row. Refuses NOT_OWN_STORE for another app's row, the shared catalog, or projected/shadow ghosts. Refuses if the template canonical plane is still pim (use dc__pim_upsert with publish_state=revoked instead). Refuses if live relationship edges or pending template binding proposals reference the row. Stamps platform_audit_log (event_type pim_legacy_row_revoked) before the publish_state flip; a failed stamp aborts the revoke.
- `dc__catalog_write` — Governed catalog write: resolve/create a brand entity, dedup-resolve or mint a product (opaque prd_ UID), and link manufactured_by. Fail-closed — refuses to create unless a ratified match rule exists for the product class. App may NOT set sku/id.
- `dc__catalog_match_rule_get` — Get the ratified catalog match rule for a product class/template. Returns fail_closed=true when no rule is configured (writes for that class are refused).
- `dc__list_catalog_match_rules` — List all configured catalog match rules (platform-scoped). Empty = fail-closed for every class.
- `dc__entity_list` — List Entity Registry instances linked to this app (entity_app_links). Cross-app instances are hidden unless granted. Specs/PIM catalog are separate (dc__pim_*).
- `dc__entity_get` — Get a single Entity Registry instance by id. Requires an active entity_app_links grant for this app; otherwise returns cross_app_read_denied (403).
- `dc__entity_delete` — Delete an entity by id from the Entity Registry.
- `dc__entity_link_app` — Grant another app an active entity_app_links row so it can read the entity. Caller must already govern the entity (own a link). Audited.
- `dc__entity_unlink_app` — Revoke an entity_app_links grant. Caller may unlink itself, or a governing app may revoke another app's grant.
- `dc__iot_device_list` — List IoT devices from iot-shack. Supports pagination and filtering by status and type.
- `dc__iot_device_get` — Get a single IoT device by deviceId from iot-shack.
- `dc__iot_device_bind_subject` — Bind an app-owned IoT device to a governed observation subject (any kind: vehicle, animal, parcel, …). Required before dc__iot_telemetry_query. Marker-law apps must pass attributes.test_marker.
- `dc__iot_device_unbind_subject` — Clear the subject bind on an app-owned device. Revokes readability via dc__iot_telemetry_query for that subject.
- `dc__iot_telemetry_query` — Read Timescale telemetry for devices currently bound to a subject this app governs. Does not accept raw deviceId alone — bind first. Cross-app subjects are denied. Unbound devices return empty.
- `dc__dam_list` — List DAM assets for the current app. Supports pagination and status filtering. Member-role callers see only their own uploads (scoped by uploaded_by). Staff / App JWT callers see all app assets.
- `dc__dam_get` — Get a single DAM asset by id.
- `dc__asset_get` — Get a single asset by id from the Entity Registry (asset-app-api).
- `dc__asset_delete` — Delete an asset by id from the Entity Registry (asset-app-api).
- `dc__delete_memory` — Delete a memory entry by memory_id from the entity_memories collection.
- `dc__deploy_status` — Returns sandbox and production frontend deploy state for this app, including URLs, deploy versions, and last_deployed_at timestamps.
- `dc__list_build_sessions` — List build sessions for this app. Shows the sequence of MCP tool calls made during app development, grouped by session (5-min inactivity gap = new session).
- `dc__provision_app_repo` — Provision a new GitHub repository for this app from the DevCockpit template. Creates repo with bootstrap files (.env.template, DEVCOCKPIT.md, .cursor/skills/devcockpit-platform.md). Returns clone URL and next steps.
- `dc__store_app_secret` — Store a secret in Vault for this app only. Path is secret/devcockpit/{env}/{tenant}/app-{app_id}/{key} (legacy secret/app/<app_id>/<key> is read as fallback). The app_id is taken from your MCP session — never from arguments. GitHub keys accept repo:write or app:configure; other keys require app:configure. Values are never echoed back. A Vault 403 is a policy denial on the gateway service token, not a bad PAT.
- `dc__get_app_secret` — Read a secret from Vault for this app only (canonical secret/devcockpit/{env}/{tenant}/app-{app_id}/{key}; legacy secret/app/<app_id>/<key> is fallback). The app_id is the MCP session — never an argument. GitHub keys accept repo:write or app:configure. Credential-class keys return present, last4, and vault_path only — never plaintext. Non-credential keys return the value. Returns found:false when absent. A Vault 403 is a policy denial, not a bad PAT.
- `dc__ops_store_app_secret` — Operator tool: store a Vault secret for any app in this tenant. Requires platform:ops. Identify the live receiving seat first — refuses inactive/deleted targets unless allow_inactive_target=true. Values are never echoed back. Never hand off bare local filesystem paths across seats. Verification from the consuming seat: their dc__get_app_secret must return found:true before the handoff is done.
- `dc__ops_get_app_secret` — Operator tool: read a Vault secret for any app in this tenant. Requires platform:ops. Use to prove a cross-seat handoff artifact is pullable before posting mailbox instructions.
- `dc__provision_app_github` — One-time app setup: store THIS app's GitHub token and repo coordinates in Vault (secret/devcockpit/{env}/{tenant}/app-{app_id}/github_*) and persist repo metadata in config_settings. Requires repo:write (app:configure also accepted). github_repo_url may include or omit a .git suffix. Token is never returned. A Vault 403 means the gateway service token lacks policy on the app secret path — not a bad PAT.
- `dc__get_build_patterns` — Get distilled build patterns from past sessions. Shows common tool sequences, frequent errors, template usage, and recommended build approaches grouped by domain (livestock, crops, equipment, sensors, tracking). Use to understand best practices from previous builds.
- `dc__list_dataflows` — List data flows — user-created and system pipelines. Shows how data moves through the platform including IoT telemetry, API ingest, MCP audit, PIM/DAM, shadow replication, and build session learning pipelines.
- `dc__get_dataflow_status` — Get operational status of all data pipelines — shows throughput, errors, and health for IoT, API ingest, MCP audit, and PIM/DAM flows. Includes device counts, pipeline health, credential status, and data volumes.
- `dc__repo_push` — Commit and push files to this app's GitHub repository. Provide full file contents (not diffs). Max 100 files per commit; request body up to ~5MB (code-content profile — TS/JSX not WAF-blocked). Requires repo:write scope. Prefer this over direct git for app-owned repos.
- `dc__repo_pull` — Read files or list the tree from this app's GitHub repository. Provide paths[] to get file contents, or omit for a tree listing. Requires repo:read scope.
- `dc__repo_status` — Get this app's GitHub repository status: HEAD commit, branch info, and whether the repo is empty. Reads THIS app's github_token from Vault. Requires repo:read scope. A Vault 403 is a policy denial on the gateway token, not a bad PAT.
- `dc__repo_delete` — Delete files from this app's GitHub repository by creating a commit that removes them. Requires repo:write scope.
- `dc__refresh_app_rules` — Regenerate and push .cursor/rules/devcockpit-integration.mdc and docs/devcockpit-deployment.md from the current platform manifest. Requires repo:write scope.
- `dc_fix__diagnose_and_fix` — Report a platform bug and have it automatically diagnosed, fixed, deployed, and verified by a cloud agent. Returns a fix_id immediately; poll dc_fix__check_status for progress. Use when an MCP tool call fails with 500 or returns wrong data. Do NOT use for missing features — only bugs in existing tools.
- `dc_fix__resolve_config` — Resolve a non-code platform issue: missing scope, missing allowlist entry, missing template binding, config error, or stale data. Spawns a cloud agent focused on config_settings. Use when a tool returns 403 insufficient_scope, tool_not_allowed, template_not_found, or a config-related error — NOT for 500 code bugs.
- `dc_fix__check_status` — Check status of a previously reported fix by fix_id. Returns current status (running, deployed, no_fix, error) and result details.
- `dc_fix__list_recent_fixes` — List recent automated fix attempts for this platform instance.
- `dc__list_app_dependencies` — Lists MCP tools this app has called in the last 30 days (from mcp_events), with call counts and production status. Defaults to the calling app when app_id is omitted.
- `dc__list_tool_dependents` — Lists apps that depend on a given MCP tool (last 30 days), including production deploy status and call counts.
- `dc__check_tool_contracts` — Runs live contract tests against platform MCP tools using a dedicated test app context. Returns pass/fail report for response field contracts.

